//@ run-pass
//@ known-failure: handlers reach no backend. `handle` parses and type-checks, and
//@   ExprHandle occurs zero times in ir/, native/ and enir/ (control: ExprCall 23).
//@   Madaros erases the whole expression — compiles, exits 0, prints nothing;
//@   lean_single refuses with E200 because the seed does not know the keyword.
//@   Evidence: docs/audit/HANDLE_EXECUTION_WITNESS_2026-08-19.md, SOUNIO-SPEC-07 §7.3.
//@ expect-stdout: handler: PASS
//@ description: a handler must discharge IO within its body and the body must run
//@ semantic-lane: SPEC-07-HANDLER-DISCHARGE

// WHY THIS FILE FAILS ON PURPOSE
//
// It previously read, in full:
//
//     fn main() -> i64 with IO, Mut, Panic, Div {
//         println("handler: PASS")
//         return 0
//     }
//
// with the comment "Rewritten to pass lean_single (handle<IO> not supported;
// prints directly)". The rewrite was honest in its comment and false in its
// name: the file asserted a string while claiming to test handler discharge, and
// the suite counted it green. Founder ruling 2026-08-19: reclassify.
//
// The assertion below is the one the file's name always claimed. It fails on
// BOTH engines today, for two different reasons, and it discriminates:
//
//   Madaros      compiles, exits 0, prints NOTHING — the handle is erased, so
//                expect-stdout is not satisfied
//   lean_single  error[E200]: undefined identifier `handle`
//
// The day handlers acquire a backend this test passes, the XPASS gate (#1910)
// fires on the stale known-failure, and the repository reports the blocker
// closed without anyone asking it to.

fn main() -> i64 with IO, Mut, Panic, Div {
    handle<IO> {
        println("handler: PASS")
    }
    return 0
}
